(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("gcp",{"gcp/google-cloud-organization-projects-and-billing":{"html":"\u003cp>Before creating any server, learn the tree that holds it. Google Cloud&#39;s hierarchy decides who can do what, where policies apply and who receives the bill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Organization, folders and projects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Project id, name and number\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>IAM inheritance and billing accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Budgets and labels\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-hierarchy\">The hierarchy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>organization\u003c/strong> (tied to a Cloud Identity or Workspace domain) contains \u003cstrong>folders\u003c/strong> (for teams or environments) that contain \u003cstrong>projects\u003c/strong>. A project holds resources: virtual machines, buckets, databases. IAM policies and \u003cstrong>organization policies\u003c/strong> set higher up are \u003cstrong>inherited\u003c/strong> by everything below.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Organization\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Company-wide policies and admins\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Folder\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Team, business unit or environment\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Project\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One application in one environment\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Resource\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A VM, bucket, topic\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A project has a \u003cstrong>name\u003c/strong> (editable), an \u003cstrong>id\u003c/strong> (globally unique, permanent) and a \u003cstrong>number\u003c/strong>. Use the id in commands and code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"billing\">Billing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>billing account\u003c/strong> pays for projects linked to it. Set \u003cstrong>budgets\u003c/strong> with alerts at 50, 90 and 100 percent, and export billing data to BigQuery for analysis. Use \u003cstrong>labels\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>env=prod\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>team=payments\u003c/code>) to split costs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud projects create acme-pay-prod --folder=123456789\ngcloud billing projects link acme-pay-prod --billing-account=ABCDEF-123456-7890AB\ngcloud services enable compute.googleapis.com --project=acme-pay-prod\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud projects create acme-pay-prod --folder=123456789\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud billing projects \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">link\u003c/span> acme-pay-prod --billing-account=ABCDEF-123456-7890AB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud services \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> compute.googleapis.com --project=acme-pay-prod\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>APIs are \u003cstrong>off by default\u003c/strong> and enabled per project.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"org&gt;fold;fold&gt;dev;fold&gt;prod;bill&gt;prod\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The Google Cloud hierarchy\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"org\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Organization\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fold\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Folders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dev project\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Prod project\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bill\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Billing account\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Organization to Folders; 2, Folders to Dev project; 3, Folders to Prod project; 4, Billing account to Prod project.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"org\">\u003cdt>Organization \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Top of the tree; company policies live here.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A policy set here applies everywhere.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fold\">\u003cdt>Folders \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Group projects by team or environment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deep folder trees get hard to manage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Dev project \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Test resources with relaxed access.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shared with prod it risks production data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Prod project \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Production resources with strict access.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Broad roles here widen the blast radius.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bill\">\u003cdt>Billing account \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pays for linked projects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No budget means surprise bills.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-cleanup-in-the-wrong-place\">Incident: Cleanup in the wrong place\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A script meant for dev deleted production disks because both lived in one project.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute disks delete prod-db-disk --project acme-app\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute disks delete prod-db-disk --project acme-app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Deleted [prod-db-disk]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">project acme-app contains dev, test and prod resources\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One project mixed all environments. The team created a project per environment, gave the cleanup tool permissions only in dev, and set budgets and alerts per project.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One project for every environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Primitive roles such as Owner given widely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No budget alert\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using project names instead of ids in scripts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-hierarchy","text":"The hierarchy"},{"id":"billing","text":"Billing"},{"id":"incident-cleanup-in-the-wrong-place","text":"Incident: Cleanup in the wrong place"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Resource hierarchy","u":"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy"},{"t":"Cloud Billing budgets","u":"https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets"}],"text":"Before creating any server, learn the tree that holds it. Google Cloud&#39;s hierarchy decides who can do what, where policies apply and who receives the bill. What you will learn Organization, folders and projects Project id, name and number IAM inheritance and billing accounts Budgets and labels The hierarchy An organization (tied to a Cloud Identity or Workspace domain) contains folders (for teams or environments) that contain projects . A project holds resources: virtual machines, buckets, databases. IAM policies and organization policies set higher up are inherited by everything below. Level Typical use Organization Company-wide policies and admins Folder Team, business unit or environment Project One application in one environment Resource A VM, bucket, topic A project has a name (editable), an id (globally unique, permanent) and a number . Use the id in commands and code. Billing A billing account pays for projects linked to it. Set budgets with alerts at 50, 90 and 100 percent, and export billing data to BigQuery for analysis. Use labels ( env=prod , team=payments ) to split costs. $ gcloud projects create acme-pay-prod --folder=123456789 $ gcloud billing projects link acme-pay-prod --billing-account=ABCDEF-123456-7890AB $ gcloud services enable compute.googleapis.com --project=acme-pay-prod APIs are off by default and enabled per project. The Google Cloud hierarchy Flow in order: 1, Organization to Folders; 2, Folders to Dev project; 3, Folders to Prod project; 4, Billing account to Prod project. Step Data store Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Organization Step What it does: Top of the tree; company policies live here. If it fails or is weak: A policy set here applies everywhere. Folders Step What it does: Group projects by team or environment. If it fails or is weak: Deep folder trees get hard to manage. Dev project Data store What it does: Test resources with relaxed access. If it fails or is weak: Shared with prod it risks production data. Prod project Data store What it does: Production resources with strict access. If it fails or is weak: Broad roles here widen the blast radius. Billing account Security What it does: Pays for linked projects. If it fails or is weak: No budget means surprise bills. Incident: Cleanup in the wrong place Incident A script meant for dev deleted production disks because both lived in one project. $ gcloud compute disks delete prod-db-disk --project acme-app Deleted [prod-db-disk] project acme-app contains dev, test and prod resources One project mixed all environments. The team created a project per environment, gave the cleanup tool permissions only in dev, and set budgets and alerts per project. Common mistakes One project for every environment Primitive roles such as Owner given widely No budget alert Using project names instead of ids in scripts"},"gcp/gcloud-cli-configurations-and-automation":{"html":"\u003cp>Almost everything you do in the console can be scripted. A few habits around configurations, credentials and output formatting keep your scripts safe and your commands readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Install, login and configurations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Credentials for people and for code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Formats, filters and queries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe scripting habits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"setup\">Setup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud auth login\ngcloud config configurations create prod\ngcloud config set project acme-pay-prod\ngcloud config set compute/region europe-west1\ngcloud config configurations list\ngcloud config configurations activate dev\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud auth login\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud config configurations create prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud config \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> project acme-pay-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud config \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> compute/region europe-west1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud config configurations list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME  IS_ACTIVE  ACCOUNT           PROJECT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dev   False      me@acme.example   acme-pay-dev\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">prod  True       me@acme.example   acme-pay-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud config configurations activate dev\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>configuration\u003c/strong> stores account, project, region and zone. Keep one per project and make the active one visible in your prompt. Cloud Shell in the browser has the CLI ready.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"credentials\">Credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>gcloud auth login\u003c/code> signs in \u003cstrong>you\u003c/strong> for CLI commands.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>gcloud auth application-default login\u003c/code> creates \u003cstrong>Application Default Credentials\u003c/strong> that client libraries and Terraform use locally.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>On Google Cloud, code uses the \u003cstrong>attached service account\u003c/strong> with no key file.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prefer \u003cstrong>impersonation\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>--impersonate-service-account=deployer@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\u003c/code> runs a command as that account without downloading a key.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"output\">Output\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute instances list --filter=&quot;labels.env=prod AND status=RUNNING&quot; \\\n    --format=&quot;table(name,zone.basename(),networkInterfaces[0].networkIP)&quot;\ngcloud compute instances describe web-1 --zone europe-west1-b --format=&quot;value(status)&quot;\ngcloud run services list --format=json | jq '.[].metadata.name'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instances list --filter=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels.env=prod AND status=RUNNING&quot;\u003c/span> \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --format=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;table(name,zone.basename(),networkInterfaces[0].networkIP)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instances describe web-1 --zone europe-west1-b --format=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;value(status)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud run services list --format=json | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.[].metadata.name&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-scripts\">Safe scripts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pass \u003ccode>--project\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--quiet\u003c/code> explicitly, use \u003ccode>set -euo pipefail\u003c/code>, run a list command first and review it before delete, and prefer Terraform for anything long-lived.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"me&gt;cfg;cfg&gt;imp;imp&gt;api;adc&gt;api\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where gcloud gets its identity\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"me\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">You\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cfg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Configuration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"imp\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Impersonation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Google Cloud APIs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"adc\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application default credentials\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, You to Configuration; 2, Configuration to Impersonation; 3, Impersonation to Google Cloud APIs; 4, Application default credentials to Google Cloud APIs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"me\">\u003cdt>You \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sign in with gcloud auth login.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A shared login hides who did what.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cfg\">\u003cdt>Configuration \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds account, project and region.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A forgotten active project targets the wrong place.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"imp\">\u003cdt>Impersonation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run as a service account without a key file.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too powerful a service account negates the safety.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>Google Cloud APIs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receive the call and check IAM.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Disabled APIs reject the call.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"adc\">\u003cdt>Application default credentials \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Used by libraries and Terraform locally.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A downloaded key file can leak.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-deleted-in-the-wrong-project\">Incident: Deleted in the wrong project\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A delete ran against production because an old configuration was still active.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud config list\ngcloud compute instances delete batch-1 --zone europe-west1-b\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud config list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[core] project = acme-pay-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instances delete batch-1 --zone europe-west1-b\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Deleted [batch-1]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The engineer believed the CLI pointed at dev. The team added the project to the shell prompt, put --project in scripts and used one configuration per environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Relying on whichever project happens to be active\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Downloading service account key files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parsing human text output instead of --format\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scripts without a dry run or list step\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"setup","text":"Setup"},{"id":"credentials","text":"Credentials"},{"id":"output","text":"Output"},{"id":"safe-scripts","text":"Safe scripts"},{"id":"incident-deleted-in-the-wrong-project","text":"Incident: Deleted in the wrong project"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"gcloud CLI overview","u":"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud"},{"t":"Application Default Credentials","u":"https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/application-default-credentials"}],"text":"Almost everything you do in the console can be scripted. A few habits around configurations, credentials and output formatting keep your scripts safe and your commands readable. What you will learn Install, login and configurations Credentials for people and for code Formats, filters and queries Safe scripting habits Setup $ gcloud auth login $ gcloud config configurations create prod $ gcloud config set project acme-pay-prod $ gcloud config set compute/region europe-west1 $ gcloud config configurations list NAME IS_ACTIVE ACCOUNT PROJECT dev False me@acme.example acme-pay-dev prod True me@acme.example acme-pay-prod $ gcloud config configurations activate dev A configuration stores account, project, region and zone. Keep one per project and make the active one visible in your prompt. Cloud Shell in the browser has the CLI ready. Credentials gcloud auth login signs in you for CLI commands. gcloud auth application-default login creates Application Default Credentials that client libraries and Terraform use locally. On Google Cloud, code uses the attached service account with no key file. Prefer impersonation : --impersonate-service-account=deployer@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com runs a command as that account without downloading a key. Output $ gcloud compute instances list --filter= \"labels.env=prod AND status=RUNNING\" \\ --format= \"table(name,zone.basename(),networkInterfaces[0].networkIP)\" $ gcloud compute instances describe web-1 --zone europe-west1-b --format= \"value(status)\" $ gcloud run services list --format=json | jq &#x27;.[].metadata.name&#x27; Safe scripts Pass --project and --quiet explicitly, use set -euo pipefail , run a list command first and review it before delete, and prefer Terraform for anything long-lived. Where gcloud gets its identity Flow in order: 1, You to Configuration; 2, Configuration to Impersonation; 3, Impersonation to Google Cloud APIs; 4, Application default credentials to Google Cloud APIs. Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. You Step What it does: Sign in with gcloud auth login. If it fails or is weak: A shared login hides who did what. Configuration Step What it does: Holds account, project and region. If it fails or is weak: A forgotten active project targets the wrong place. Impersonation Security What it does: Run as a service account without a key file. If it fails or is weak: Too powerful a service account negates the safety. Google Cloud APIs Step What it does: Receive the call and check IAM. If it fails or is weak: Disabled APIs reject the call. Application default credentials Security What it does: Used by libraries and Terraform locally. If it fails or is weak: A downloaded key file can leak. Incident: Deleted in the wrong project Incident A delete ran against production because an old configuration was still active. $ gcloud config list [core] project = acme-pay-prod $ gcloud compute instances delete batch-1 --zone europe-west1-b Deleted [batch-1] The engineer believed the CLI pointed at dev. The team added the project to the shell prompt, put --project in scripts and used one configuration per environment. Common mistakes Relying on whichever project happens to be active Downloading service account key files Parsing human text output instead of --format Scripts without a dry run or list step"},"gcp/vpc-networks-subnets-and-firewall-rules":{"html":"\u003cp>Networking in Google Cloud differs from other clouds in one big way: a VPC spans all regions. Learning that, and how firewall rules attach to instances, gives you a clean and safe design.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Global VPCs and regional subnets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Firewall rules and priorities\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud NAT and private access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared VPC and peering\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vpc-and-subnets\">VPC and subnets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>VPC network\u003c/strong> is global; each \u003cstrong>subnet\u003c/strong> belongs to one region and has an IP range. Use \u003cstrong>custom mode\u003c/strong> so you pick the ranges rather than accepting the auto-mode defaults, and plan non-overlapping ranges for peering and on-premises links.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute networks create prod-vpc --subnet-mode=custom\ngcloud compute networks subnets create app-eu --network=prod-vpc \\\n    --region=europe-west1 --range=10.10.0.0/20 --enable-private-ip-google-access\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute networks create prod-vpc --subnet-mode=custom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute networks subnets create app-eu --network=prod-vpc \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --region=europe-west1 --range=10.10.0.0/20 --enable-private-ip-google-access\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"firewall-rules\">Firewall rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ingress is denied and egress allowed by default (implied rules). Add rules with a \u003cstrong>priority\u003c/strong> (lower number wins), direction, protocol, ports, source and \u003cstrong>target\u003c/strong> (tags or service accounts).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-lb-to-web --network=prod-vpc \\\n    --direction=INGRESS --allow=tcp:8080 \\\n    --source-ranges=130.211.0.0/22,35.191.0.0/16 \\\n    --target-service-accounts=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\ngcloud compute ssh web-1 --tunnel-through-iap\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-lb-to-web --network=prod-vpc \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --direction=INGRESS --allow=tcp:8080 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --source-ranges=130.211.0.0/22,35.191.0.0/16 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --target-service-accounts=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute ssh web-1 --tunnel-through-iap\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Prefer \u003cstrong>service accounts\u003c/strong> as targets because tags can be set by anyone with edit rights on the instance. \u003cstrong>IAP\u003c/strong> tunnelling gives administrators SSH without opening port 22 to the internet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reaching-out\">Reaching out\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>VMs without public IPs reach the internet through \u003cstrong>Cloud NAT\u003c/strong> (via a Cloud Router) and reach Google APIs through \u003cstrong>Private Google Access\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Shared VPC\u003c/strong> lets a host project own the network while service projects deploy into it. \u003cstrong>VPC peering\u003c/strong> connects two VPCs privately; it is not transitive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;lb;lb&gt;vm;vm&gt;nat;iap&gt;vm\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A private Google Cloud network\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Users\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vm\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Private VMs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"nat\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud NAT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"iap\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">IAP tunnel\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Users to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Private VMs; 3, Private VMs to Cloud NAT; 4, IAP tunnel to Private VMs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Users \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reach the load balancer on a public address.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An open admin port invites attacks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Terminates traffic and forwards to backends.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing health-check ranges in the firewall mark backends unhealthy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vm\">\u003cdt>Private VMs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> No public IP; rules by service account.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A broad allow rule defeats the isolation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"nat\">\u003cdt>Cloud NAT \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Outbound internet without public IPs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Port exhaustion breaks many outbound connections.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"iap\">\u003cdt>IAP tunnel \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Admin SSH without a public port.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Granting IAP tunnel access too widely replaces one open door with another.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-backends-unhealthy\">Incident: Backends unhealthy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new firewall rule set blocked the load balancer&#39;s health checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute backend-services get-health web --global\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute backend-services get-health web --global\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-1  healthState: UNHEALTHY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">firewall: no rule allowing 130.211.0.0/22 and 35.191.0.0/16 on tcp:8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The load balancer probes come from Google ranges. Allowing those ranges to the service port brought backends back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the default network in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Firewall rules based on tags that developers can edit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overlapping IP ranges between VPCs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting health-check source ranges\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"vpc-and-subnets","text":"VPC and subnets"},{"id":"firewall-rules","text":"Firewall rules"},{"id":"reaching-out","text":"Reaching out"},{"id":"incident-backends-unhealthy","text":"Incident: Backends unhealthy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"VPC overview","u":"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc"},{"t":"Firewall rules","u":"https://cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewalls"}],"text":"Networking in Google Cloud differs from other clouds in one big way: a VPC spans all regions. Learning that, and how firewall rules attach to instances, gives you a clean and safe design. What you will learn Global VPCs and regional subnets Firewall rules and priorities Cloud NAT and private access Shared VPC and peering VPC and subnets A VPC network is global; each subnet belongs to one region and has an IP range. Use custom mode so you pick the ranges rather than accepting the auto-mode defaults, and plan non-overlapping ranges for peering and on-premises links. $ gcloud compute networks create prod-vpc --subnet-mode=custom $ gcloud compute networks subnets create app-eu --network=prod-vpc \\ --region=europe-west1 --range=10.10.0.0/20 --enable-private-ip-google-access Firewall rules Ingress is denied and egress allowed by default (implied rules). Add rules with a priority (lower number wins), direction, protocol, ports, source and target (tags or service accounts). $ gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-lb-to-web --network=prod-vpc \\ --direction=INGRESS --allow=tcp:8080 \\ --source-ranges=130.211.0.0/22,35.191.0.0/16 \\ --target-service-accounts=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com $ gcloud compute ssh web-1 --tunnel-through-iap Prefer service accounts as targets because tags can be set by anyone with edit rights on the instance. IAP tunnelling gives administrators SSH without opening port 22 to the internet. Reaching out VMs without public IPs reach the internet through Cloud NAT (via a Cloud Router) and reach Google APIs through Private Google Access . Shared VPC lets a host project own the network while service projects deploy into it. VPC peering connects two VPCs privately; it is not transitive. A private Google Cloud network Flow in order: 1, Users to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Private VMs; 3, Private VMs to Cloud NAT; 4, IAP tunnel to Private VMs. Client Network edge Service Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Users Client What it does: Reach the load balancer on a public address. If it fails or is weak: An open admin port invites attacks. Load balancer Network edge What it does: Terminates traffic and forwards to backends. If it fails or is weak: Missing health-check ranges in the firewall mark backends unhealthy. Private VMs Service What it does: No public IP; rules by service account. If it fails or is weak: A broad allow rule defeats the isolation. Cloud NAT Step What it does: Outbound internet without public IPs. If it fails or is weak: Port exhaustion breaks many outbound connections. IAP tunnel Security What it does: Admin SSH without a public port. If it fails or is weak: Granting IAP tunnel access too widely replaces one open door with another. Incident: Backends unhealthy Incident A new firewall rule set blocked the load balancer&#39;s health checks. $ gcloud compute backend-services get-health web --global web-1 healthState: UNHEALTHY firewall: no rule allowing 130.211.0.0/22 and 35.191.0.0/16 on tcp:8080 The load balancer probes come from Google ranges. Allowing those ranges to the service port brought backends back. Common mistakes Using the default network in production Firewall rules based on tags that developers can edit Overlapping IP ranges between VPCs Forgetting health-check source ranges"},"gcp/compute-engine-images-and-managed-instance-groups":{"html":"\u003cp>A VM you log into and fix by hand is a pet. Google Cloud gives you the tools to run herds: templates, groups and health checks that rebuild failed machines for you.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Machine types, images and disks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Instance templates and managed instance groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Autohealing, autoscaling and rolling updates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot VMs and cost control\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"creating-a-vm\">Creating a VM\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute instances create web-1 --zone=europe-west1-b \\\n    --machine-type=e2-standard-2 --image-family=debian-12 --image-project=debian-cloud \\\n    --subnet=app-eu --no-address --service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com \\\n    --metadata=enable-oslogin=TRUE\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instances create web-1 --zone=europe-west1-b \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --machine-type=e2-standard-2 --image-family=debian-12 --image-project=debian-cloud \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --subnet=app-eu --no-address --service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --metadata=enable-oslogin=TRUE\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pick a machine family for the workload (E2 or N2 general, C for compute, M for memory, A or G for GPUs) or a custom size. Use \u003cstrong>OS Login\u003c/strong> to tie SSH access to IAM instead of copied keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"instance-templates-and-groups\">Instance templates and groups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>instance template\u003c/strong> records machine type, image, network and startup script. A \u003cstrong>managed instance group (MIG)\u003c/strong> keeps N copies of it running.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute instance-templates create web-v2 --machine-type=e2-standard-2 \\\n    --image-family=web-golden --subnet=app-eu --no-address\ngcloud compute instance-groups managed create web --region=europe-west1 \\\n    --template=web-v2 --size=3\ngcloud compute instance-groups managed set-autoscaling web --region=europe-west1 \\\n    --max-num-replicas=10 --target-cpu-utilization=0.6\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instance-templates create web-v2 --machine-type=e2-standard-2 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --image-family=web-golden --subnet=app-eu --no-address\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instance-groups managed create web --region=europe-west1 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --template=web-v2 --size=3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instance-groups managed set-autoscaling web --region=europe-west1 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --max-num-replicas=10 --target-cpu-utilization=0.6\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>regional\u003c/strong> MIG spreads instances across zones. \u003cstrong>Autohealing\u003c/strong> recreates instances that fail an application health check. \u003cstrong>Rolling updates\u003c/strong> replace instances gradually (\u003ccode>max-surge\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max-unavailable\u003c/code>) when you point the group at a new template.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost\">Cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Spot VMs\u003c/strong> cost far less but can be stopped at any moment, so use them for batch and stateless work. Rightsizing recommendations and committed use discounts reduce steady cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"img&gt;tmpl;tmpl&gt;mig;mig&gt;hc;auto&gt;mig\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A self-healing group of VMs\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"img\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Golden image\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tmpl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Instance template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mig\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Regional MIG\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hc\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Health check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"auto\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Autoscaler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Golden image to Instance template; 2, Instance template to Regional MIG; 3, Regional MIG to Health check; 4, Autoscaler to Regional MIG.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"img\">\u003cdt>Golden image \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The built operating system and app.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A manual image drifts from the code.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tmpl\">\u003cdt>Instance template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Machine type, image, network and scripts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Templates cannot be edited; make a new one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mig\">\u003cdt>Regional MIG \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keeps the wanted number of instances in several zones.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A zone with no quota leaves it short.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hc\">\u003cdt>Health check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tests the app; failing VMs are recreated.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A check that is too strict causes a restart loop.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"auto\">\u003cdt>Autoscaler \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Adds or removes instances based on load.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow startup lets a spike pass unserved.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-restart-loop\">Incident: Restart loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Autohealing kept recreating instances because the health check timed out before the app started.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances web --region=europe-west1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances web --region=europe-west1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-a1  RECREATING  UNHEALTHY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">health check: initial delay 30s; app ready after 90s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The check started before the app was ready and declared the VM unhealthy. Raising the initial delay above the startup time stopped the loop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hand-built VMs with no template\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Zonal groups for services that must survive a zone failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks with no initial delay for slow starts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot VMs for stateful services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"creating-a-vm","text":"Creating a VM"},{"id":"instance-templates-and-groups","text":"Instance templates and groups"},{"id":"cost","text":"Cost"},{"id":"incident-restart-loop","text":"Incident: Restart loop"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Compute Engine documentation","u":"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs"},{"t":"Managed instance groups","u":"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups"}],"text":"A VM you log into and fix by hand is a pet. Google Cloud gives you the tools to run herds: templates, groups and health checks that rebuild failed machines for you. What you will learn Machine types, images and disks Instance templates and managed instance groups Autohealing, autoscaling and rolling updates Spot VMs and cost control Creating a VM $ gcloud compute instances create web-1 --zone=europe-west1-b \\ --machine-type=e2-standard-2 --image-family=debian-12 --image-project=debian-cloud \\ --subnet=app-eu --no-address --service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com \\ --metadata=enable-oslogin=TRUE Pick a machine family for the workload (E2 or N2 general, C for compute, M for memory, A or G for GPUs) or a custom size. Use OS Login to tie SSH access to IAM instead of copied keys. Instance templates and groups An instance template records machine type, image, network and startup script. A managed instance group (MIG) keeps N copies of it running. $ gcloud compute instance-templates create web-v2 --machine-type=e2-standard-2 \\ --image-family=web-golden --subnet=app-eu --no-address $ gcloud compute instance-groups managed create web --region=europe-west1 \\ --template=web-v2 --size=3 $ gcloud compute instance-groups managed set-autoscaling web --region=europe-west1 \\ --max-num-replicas=10 --target-cpu-utilization=0.6 A regional MIG spreads instances across zones. Autohealing recreates instances that fail an application health check. Rolling updates replace instances gradually ( max-surge , max-unavailable ) when you point the group at a new template. Cost Spot VMs cost far less but can be stopped at any moment, so use them for batch and stateless work. Rightsizing recommendations and committed use discounts reduce steady cost. A self-healing group of VMs Flow in order: 1, Golden image to Instance template; 2, Instance template to Regional MIG; 3, Regional MIG to Health check; 4, Autoscaler to Regional MIG. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Golden image Step What it does: The built operating system and app. If it fails or is weak: A manual image drifts from the code. Instance template Step What it does: Machine type, image, network and scripts. If it fails or is weak: Templates cannot be edited; make a new one. Regional MIG Step What it does: Keeps the wanted number of instances in several zones. If it fails or is weak: A zone with no quota leaves it short. Health check Observability What it does: Tests the app; failing VMs are recreated. If it fails or is weak: A check that is too strict causes a restart loop. Autoscaler Step What it does: Adds or removes instances based on load. If it fails or is weak: Slow startup lets a spike pass unserved. Incident: Restart loop Incident Autohealing kept recreating instances because the health check timed out before the app started. $ gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances web --region=europe-west1 web-a1 RECREATING UNHEALTHY health check: initial delay 30s; app ready after 90s The check started before the app was ready and declared the VM unhealthy. Raising the initial delay above the startup time stopped the loop. Common mistakes Hand-built VMs with no template Zonal groups for services that must survive a zone failure Health checks with no initial delay for slow starts Spot VMs for stateful services"},"gcp/cloud-storage-and-persistent-disks":{"html":"\u003cp>Storage on Google Cloud comes in object, block and file forms. Most beginners need only the first two and the habits that keep data safe and cheap.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Buckets, classes and locations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Access control and signed URLs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Lifecycle, versioning and retention\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disks, snapshots and file storage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"buckets\">Buckets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud storage buckets create gs://acme-exports --location=europe-west1 \\\n    --uniform-bucket-level-access --public-access-prevention\ngcloud storage cp report.csv gs://acme-exports/2026/\ngcloud storage ls --long gs://acme-exports/2026/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud storage buckets create gs://acme-exports --location=europe-west1 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --uniform-bucket-level-access --public-access-prevention\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud storage \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> report.csv gs://acme-exports/2026/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud storage \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> --long gs://acme-exports/2026/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A bucket has a \u003cstrong>location\u003c/strong> (region, dual-region or multi-region) and a default \u003cstrong>storage class\u003c/strong>: Standard for hot data, Nearline (30 days), Coldline (90 days), Archive (365 days) for rarely read data. Colder classes cost less to keep and more to read, and have minimum storage durations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access\">Access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>uniform bucket-level access\u003c/strong> so IAM alone controls access (no per-object ACLs), turn on \u003cstrong>public access prevention\u003c/strong>, and give service accounts the narrowest role (\u003ccode>roles/storage.objectViewer\u003c/code>). For temporary sharing, create a \u003cstrong>signed URL\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifecycle-and-protection\">Lifecycle and protection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{&quot;rule&quot;: [\n  {&quot;action&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;SetStorageClass&quot;, &quot;storageClass&quot;: &quot;NEARLINE&quot;}, &quot;condition&quot;: {&quot;age&quot;: 30}},\n  {&quot;action&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;Delete&quot;}, &quot;condition&quot;: {&quot;age&quot;: 365}}\n]}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;rule&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SetStorageClass&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;storageClass&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;NEARLINE&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;condition&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;age&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Delete&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;condition&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;age&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">365\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Object versioning\u003c/strong> keeps previous versions after overwrite or delete; a \u003cstrong>retention policy\u003c/strong> prevents deletion for a period. Lifecycle rules move or delete objects automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disks-and-files\">Disks and files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>Persistent Disk\u003c/strong> (balanced, SSD) attaches to VMs and survives instance deletion if configured that way. Take scheduled \u003cstrong>snapshots\u003c/strong> and test restoring them. \u003cstrong>Hyperdisk\u003c/strong> gives tunable performance. \u003cstrong>Filestore\u003c/strong> offers shared NFS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;bkt;bkt&gt;life;bkt&gt;ver;app&gt;disk\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where data lives\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bkt\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bucket\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"life\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lifecycle rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ver\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Versioning\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"disk\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Persistent Disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Bucket; 2, Bucket to Lifecycle rules; 3, Bucket to Versioning; 4, Application to Persistent Disk.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads and writes through IAM roles.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overly broad roles expose all buckets.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bkt\">\u003cdt>Bucket \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Objects with a class and a location.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Public access exposes data to the world.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"life\">\u003cdt>Lifecycle rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Move to colder classes or delete by age.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting too soon loses needed data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ver\">\u003cdt>Versioning \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keeps older versions after change.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unbounded versions raise cost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"disk\">\u003cdt>Persistent Disk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Block storage for one VM with snapshots.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No snapshot schedule means no restore point.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-public-bucket\">Incident: The public bucket\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A bucket was made public for a quick share and customer exports stayed exposed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud storage buckets get-iam-policy gs://acme-exports\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud storage buckets get-iam-policy gs://acme-exports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">members: allUsers  role: roles/storage.objectViewer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">audit: 14,201 external reads\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A one-off share became a permanent exposure. The team removed allUsers, enabled public access prevention at the organization, and used signed URLs with a short expiry for sharing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting allUsers or allAuthenticatedUsers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Archive class for data read often\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No lifecycle rules, so old data piles up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disks without snapshot schedules\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"buckets","text":"Buckets"},{"id":"access","text":"Access"},{"id":"lifecycle-and-protection","text":"Lifecycle and protection"},{"id":"disks-and-files","text":"Disks and files"},{"id":"incident-the-public-bucket","text":"Incident: The public bucket"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cloud Storage documentation","u":"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs"},{"t":"Persistent Disk","u":"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks"}],"text":"Storage on Google Cloud comes in object, block and file forms. Most beginners need only the first two and the habits that keep data safe and cheap. What you will learn Buckets, classes and locations Access control and signed URLs Lifecycle, versioning and retention Disks, snapshots and file storage Buckets $ gcloud storage buckets create gs://acme-exports --location=europe-west1 \\ --uniform-bucket-level-access --public-access-prevention $ gcloud storage cp report.csv gs://acme-exports/2026/ $ gcloud storage ls --long gs://acme-exports/2026/ A bucket has a location (region, dual-region or multi-region) and a default storage class : Standard for hot data, Nearline (30 days), Coldline (90 days), Archive (365 days) for rarely read data. Colder classes cost less to keep and more to read, and have minimum storage durations. Access Use uniform bucket-level access so IAM alone controls access (no per-object ACLs), turn on public access prevention , and give service accounts the narrowest role ( roles/storage.objectViewer ). For temporary sharing, create a signed URL . Lifecycle and protection { \"rule\" : [ { \"action\" : { \"type\" : \"SetStorageClass\" , \"storageClass\" : \"NEARLINE\" } , \"condition\" : { \"age\" : 30 } } , { \"action\" : { \"type\" : \"Delete\" } , \"condition\" : { \"age\" : 365 } } ] } Object versioning keeps previous versions after overwrite or delete; a retention policy prevents deletion for a period. Lifecycle rules move or delete objects automatically. Disks and files A Persistent Disk (balanced, SSD) attaches to VMs and survives instance deletion if configured that way. Take scheduled snapshots and test restoring them. Hyperdisk gives tunable performance. Filestore offers shared NFS. Where data lives Flow in order: 1, Application to Bucket; 2, Bucket to Lifecycle rules; 3, Bucket to Versioning; 4, Application to Persistent Disk. Service Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Service What it does: Reads and writes through IAM roles. If it fails or is weak: Overly broad roles expose all buckets. Bucket Data store What it does: Objects with a class and a location. If it fails or is weak: Public access exposes data to the world. Lifecycle rules Step What it does: Move to colder classes or delete by age. If it fails or is weak: Deleting too soon loses needed data. Versioning Data store What it does: Keeps older versions after change. If it fails or is weak: Unbounded versions raise cost. Persistent Disk Data store What it does: Block storage for one VM with snapshots. If it fails or is weak: No snapshot schedule means no restore point. Incident: The public bucket Incident A bucket was made public for a quick share and customer exports stayed exposed. $ gcloud storage buckets get-iam-policy gs://acme-exports members: allUsers role: roles/storage.objectViewer audit: 14,201 external reads A one-off share became a permanent exposure. The team removed allUsers, enabled public access prevention at the organization, and used signed URLs with a short expiry for sharing. Common mistakes Granting allUsers or allAuthenticatedUsers Archive class for data read often No lifecycle rules, so old data piles up Disks without snapshot schedules"},"gcp/gke-autopilot-and-standard-clusters":{"html":"\u003cp>GKE removes the hardest part of Kubernetes, running the control plane, and offers two levels of further help. Choosing the right mode and securing identity early prevents most surprises.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Autopilot and Standard modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Regional clusters, release channels and upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Workload Identity and private clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ingress, Gateway and cost habits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-modes\">Two modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Autopilot\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Standard\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Nodes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Managed by Google\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>You manage node pools\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Billing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pod resource requests\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Node VMs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Privileged pods, custom kernel\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Limited\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Allowed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Hardened defaults\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>You configure\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"create-a-cluster\">Create a cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud container clusters create-auto prod --region=europe-west1 --release-channel=regular\ngcloud container clusters get-credentials prod --region=europe-west1\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud container clusters create-auto prod --region=europe-west1 --release-channel=regular\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud container clusters get-credentials prod --region=europe-west1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Regional\u003c/strong> clusters replicate the control plane across zones. A \u003cstrong>release channel\u003c/strong> (rapid, regular, stable) gets automatic upgrades; set \u003cstrong>maintenance windows\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>exclusions\u003c/strong> for busy periods.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity\">Identity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Workload Identity Federation for GKE\u003c/strong> lets a Kubernetes service account act as a Google service account, so pods get short-lived tokens without key files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl annotate serviceaccount web -n shop \\\n    iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl annotate serviceaccount web -n shop \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>private clusters\u003c/strong> (nodes without public IPs) and authorised networks for the API endpoint.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-and-cost\">Traffic and cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Expose services through the \u003cstrong>Gateway API\u003c/strong> or Ingress to a Google load balancer. In Autopilot, set accurate resource \u003cstrong>requests\u003c/strong>, since they decide the bill. In Standard, use cluster autoscaler, Spot node pools for tolerant workloads and bin packing to avoid idle nodes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"dev&gt;cp;cp&gt;pool;wi&gt;pool;pool&gt;lb\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A GKE cluster\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubectl and CI\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Managed control plane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pool\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wi\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workload Identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Google load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, kubectl and CI to Managed control plane; 2, Managed control plane to Nodes; 3, Workload Identity to Nodes; 4, Nodes to Google load balancer.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>kubectl and CI \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Talks to the Kubernetes API.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A public API with no network limits is exposed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cp\">\u003cdt>Managed control plane \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run by Google; regional for high availability.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Version skew blocks old APIs after upgrade.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pool\">\u003cdt>Nodes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Autopilot-managed or your node pools.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Idle nodes cost money in Standard.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wi\">\u003cdt>Workload Identity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Maps a Kubernetes service account to a Google one.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it pods share the node account.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Google load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends external traffic to pods via Gateway.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong health-check path marks pods unhealthy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-pods-stuck-pending\">Incident: Pods stuck pending\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>New pods stayed pending because requests exceeded what the cluster could provide in Standard mode.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod web-7d9f\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod web-7d9f\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Warning FailedScheduling  0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cluster-autoscaler: node pool at maximum size 3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The node pool maximum was too low for the new requests. Raising the maximum and rightsizing requests let pods schedule. The team later moved the workload to Autopilot to remove node sizing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using node service account keys instead of Workload Identity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Requests copied from examples, inflating the bill in Autopilot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling auto-upgrades and falling far behind\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A public API endpoint with no authorised networks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-modes","text":"Two modes"},{"id":"create-a-cluster","text":"Create a cluster"},{"id":"identity","text":"Identity"},{"id":"traffic-and-cost","text":"Traffic and cost"},{"id":"incident-pods-stuck-pending","text":"Incident: Pods stuck pending"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GKE overview","u":"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview"},{"t":"Autopilot overview","u":"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/autopilot-overview"}],"text":"GKE removes the hardest part of Kubernetes, running the control plane, and offers two levels of further help. Choosing the right mode and securing identity early prevents most surprises. What you will learn Autopilot and Standard modes Regional clusters, release channels and upgrades Workload Identity and private clusters Ingress, Gateway and cost habits Two modes Autopilot Standard Nodes Managed by Google You manage node pools Billing Pod resource requests Node VMs Privileged pods, custom kernel Limited Allowed Hardened defaults Yes You configure Create a cluster $ gcloud container clusters create-auto prod --region=europe-west1 --release-channel=regular $ gcloud container clusters get-credentials prod --region=europe-west1 $ kubectl get nodes Regional clusters replicate the control plane across zones. A release channel (rapid, regular, stable) gets automatic upgrades; set maintenance windows and exclusions for busy periods. Identity Workload Identity Federation for GKE lets a Kubernetes service account act as a Google service account, so pods get short-lived tokens without key files. $ kubectl annotate serviceaccount web -n shop \\ iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com Use private clusters (nodes without public IPs) and authorised networks for the API endpoint. Traffic and cost Expose services through the Gateway API or Ingress to a Google load balancer. In Autopilot, set accurate resource requests , since they decide the bill. In Standard, use cluster autoscaler, Spot node pools for tolerant workloads and bin packing to avoid idle nodes. A GKE cluster Flow in order: 1, kubectl and CI to Managed control plane; 2, Managed control plane to Nodes; 3, Workload Identity to Nodes; 4, Nodes to Google load balancer. Step Service Security Network edge Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. kubectl and CI Step What it does: Talks to the Kubernetes API. If it fails or is weak: A public API with no network limits is exposed. Managed control plane Step What it does: Run by Google; regional for high availability. If it fails or is weak: Version skew blocks old APIs after upgrade. Nodes Service What it does: Autopilot-managed or your node pools. If it fails or is weak: Idle nodes cost money in Standard. Workload Identity Security What it does: Maps a Kubernetes service account to a Google one. If it fails or is weak: Without it pods share the node account. Google load balancer Network edge What it does: Sends external traffic to pods via Gateway. If it fails or is weak: Wrong health-check path marks pods unhealthy. Incident: Pods stuck pending Incident New pods stayed pending because requests exceeded what the cluster could provide in Standard mode. $ kubectl describe pod web-7d9f Warning FailedScheduling 0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu. cluster-autoscaler: node pool at maximum size 3 The node pool maximum was too low for the new requests. Raising the maximum and rightsizing requests let pods schedule. The team later moved the workload to Autopilot to remove node sizing. Common mistakes Using node service account keys instead of Workload Identity Requests copied from examples, inflating the bill in Autopilot Disabling auto-upgrades and falling far behind A public API endpoint with no authorised networks"},"gcp/cloud-run-and-cloud-functions":{"html":"\u003cp>For many web services and background tasks, a container platform that charges per use is simpler than servers or Kubernetes. Cloud Run is Google Cloud&#39;s answer, and it is often the right first choice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deploying services and revisions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scaling, concurrency and cold starts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets, networking and authentication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Jobs and event-driven functions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deploy\">Deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud run deploy web --image=europe-west1-docker.pkg.dev/acme-pay-prod/app/web:1.4.2 \\\n    --region=europe-west1 --service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com \\\n    --no-allow-unauthenticated --max-instances=20 --concurrency=80 \\\n    --set-secrets=DB_PASSWORD=db-password:latest\ngcloud run services update-traffic web --region=europe-west1 --to-revisions=web-00007=10,web-00006=90\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud run deploy web --image=europe-west1-docker.pkg.dev/acme-pay-prod/app/web:1.4.2 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --region=europe-west1 --service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --no-allow-unauthenticated --max-instances=20 --concurrency=80 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --set-secrets=DB_PASSWORD=db-password:latest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud run services update-traffic web --region=europe-west1 --to-revisions=web-00007=10,web-00006=90\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each deploy makes a \u003cstrong>revision\u003c/strong>. Traffic can split between revisions for canary tests and rolled back instantly. The container must listen on the port in \u003ccode>$PORT\u003c/code> and keep no local state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scaling\">Scaling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An instance handles many requests at once up to \u003ccode>--concurrency\u003c/code>. Cloud Run adds instances when concurrency fills and scales to \u003cstrong>zero\u003c/strong> when idle. \u003ccode>--min-instances\u003c/code> keeps warm instances; \u003ccode>--max-instances\u003c/code> protects databases from overload. Choose CPU always allocated for background work, or CPU only during requests to save money.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security-and-networking\">Security and networking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Services are private unless you grant \u003ccode>roles/run.invoker\u003c/code>; use \u003ccode>--no-allow-unauthenticated\u003c/code> and call with identity tokens. Read secrets from \u003cstrong>Secret Manager\u003c/strong>. Use \u003cstrong>Direct VPC egress\u003c/strong> to reach private resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"jobs-and-functions\">Jobs and functions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Cloud Run jobs\u003c/strong> run containers to completion on a schedule or on demand (batch, migrations). \u003cstrong>Cloud Run functions\u003c/strong> run small event handlers triggered by HTTP, Pub/Sub, Cloud Storage and others, built on the same platform.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;run;run&gt;rev;rev&gt;db;sec&gt;run\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Cloud Run request path\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Caller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud Run service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Revisions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Secret Manager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Caller to Cloud Run service; 2, Cloud Run service to Revisions; 3, Revisions to Database; 4, Secret Manager to Cloud Run service.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Caller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends a request with an identity token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Allowing unauthenticated calls exposes the service.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Cloud Run service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Routes to a revision and scales instances.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cold starts delay the first request.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Revisions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Versions with traffic split.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old revisions linger if never cleaned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Secret Manager \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Supplies passwords at runtime.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hard-coded secrets leak in images.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limited by max instances and connections.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unlimited scale exhausts connections.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-database-connection-storm\">Incident: Database connection storm\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cloud Run scaled quickly and opened more connections than the database allowed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud run services describe web --format='value(spec.template.scaling.maxInstance)'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud run services describe web --format=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;value(spec.template.scaling.maxInstance)&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(none)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">postgres: FATAL: remaining connection slots are reserved\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Unlimited scaling met a limited database. The team set max instances, used a connection pooler and lowered per-instance connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No maximum instances in front of a database\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing state in container local disk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Public services without authentication needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets in environment variables baked into the image\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"deploy","text":"Deploy"},{"id":"scaling","text":"Scaling"},{"id":"security-and-networking","text":"Security and networking"},{"id":"jobs-and-functions","text":"Jobs and functions"},{"id":"incident-database-connection-storm","text":"Incident: Database connection storm"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cloud Run documentation","u":"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs"},{"t":"Cloud Run functions","u":"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs"}],"text":"For many web services and background tasks, a container platform that charges per use is simpler than servers or Kubernetes. Cloud Run is Google Cloud&#39;s answer, and it is often the right first choice. What you will learn Deploying services and revisions Scaling, concurrency and cold starts Secrets, networking and authentication Jobs and event-driven functions Deploy $ gcloud run deploy web --image=europe-west1-docker.pkg.dev/acme-pay-prod/app/web:1.4.2 \\ --region=europe-west1 --service-account=web@acme-pay-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com \\ --no-allow-unauthenticated --max-instances=20 --concurrency=80 \\ --set-secrets=DB_PASSWORD=db-password:latest $ gcloud run services update-traffic web --region=europe-west1 --to-revisions=web-00007=10,web-00006=90 Each deploy makes a revision . Traffic can split between revisions for canary tests and rolled back instantly. The container must listen on the port in $PORT and keep no local state. Scaling An instance handles many requests at once up to --concurrency . Cloud Run adds instances when concurrency fills and scales to zero when idle. --min-instances keeps warm instances; --max-instances protects databases from overload. Choose CPU always allocated for background work, or CPU only during requests to save money. Security and networking Services are private unless you grant roles/run.invoker ; use --no-allow-unauthenticated and call with identity tokens. Read secrets from Secret Manager . Use Direct VPC egress to reach private resources. Jobs and functions Cloud Run jobs run containers to completion on a schedule or on demand (batch, migrations). Cloud Run functions run small event handlers triggered by HTTP, Pub/Sub, Cloud Storage and others, built on the same platform. Cloud Run request path Flow in order: 1, Caller to Cloud Run service; 2, Cloud Run service to Revisions; 3, Revisions to Database; 4, Secret Manager to Cloud Run service. Step Service Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Caller Step What it does: Sends a request with an identity token. If it fails or is weak: Allowing unauthenticated calls exposes the service. Cloud Run service Service What it does: Routes to a revision and scales instances. If it fails or is weak: Cold starts delay the first request. Revisions Step What it does: Versions with traffic split. If it fails or is weak: Old revisions linger if never cleaned. Secret Manager Security What it does: Supplies passwords at runtime. If it fails or is weak: Hard-coded secrets leak in images. Database Data store What it does: Limited by max instances and connections. If it fails or is weak: Unlimited scale exhausts connections. Incident: Database connection storm Incident Cloud Run scaled quickly and opened more connections than the database allowed. $ gcloud run services describe web --format= &#x27;value(spec.template.scaling.maxInstance)&#x27; (none) postgres: FATAL: remaining connection slots are reserved Unlimited scaling met a limited database. The team set max instances, used a connection pooler and lowered per-instance connections. Common mistakes No maximum instances in front of a database Storing state in container local disk Public services without authentication needs Secrets in environment variables baked into the image"},"gcp/cloud-sql-spanner-bigquery-and-firestore":{"html":"\u003cp>Choosing the wrong database is expensive to reverse. A short map of what each Google Cloud database is good at, plus safe defaults for the common one, prevents most regret.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The database map\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud SQL high availability and backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Connecting privately\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>BigQuery cost control\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-map\">The map\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Choose\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Normal relational app\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cloud SQL (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Heavy PostgreSQL, analytics mix\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>AlloyDB\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Globally consistent relational, huge scale\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Spanner\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Documents, mobile and web\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Firestore\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Very high-throughput key-value\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bigtable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Analytics over large data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>BigQuery\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cloud-sql-safely\">Cloud SQL safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud sql instances create orders-db --database-version=POSTGRES_16 --tier=db-custom-4-16384 \\\n    --region=europe-west1 --availability-type=REGIONAL --network=prod-vpc --no-assign-ip \\\n    --backup-start-time=02:00 --enable-point-in-time-recovery\ngcloud sql connect orders-db --user=app\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud sql instances create orders-db --database-version=POSTGRES_16 --tier=db-custom-4-16384 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --region=europe-west1 --availability-type=REGIONAL --network=prod-vpc --no-assign-ip \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --backup-start-time=02:00 --enable-point-in-time-recovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud sql connect orders-db --user=app\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>High availability\u003c/strong> keeps a standby in another zone with automatic failover. \u003cstrong>Backups plus point-in-time recovery\u003c/strong> let you restore to a moment; practise a restore. Use \u003cstrong>private IP\u003c/strong>, and the \u003cstrong>Cloud SQL Auth Proxy\u003c/strong> or connectors for IAM-based encrypted connections. Add \u003cstrong>read replicas\u003c/strong> for read scaling, which are not a substitute for HA.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bigquery\">BigQuery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>BigQuery separates storage from compute and bills by data scanned (on-demand) or by reserved capacity. \u003cstrong>Partition\u003c/strong> tables by date, \u003cstrong>cluster\u003c/strong> by common filters, select only needed columns and use a dry run.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bq query --dry_run --use_legacy_sql=false 'select user_id from logs.events where dt = &quot;2026-11-05&quot;'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bq query --dry_run --use_legacy_sql=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;select user_id from logs.events where dt = &quot;2026-11-05&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Query successfully validated. This query will process 3.1 GB when run.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"app&gt;sql;sql&gt;bk;app&gt;sp;app&gt;bq\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Picking and protecting a database\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sql\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud SQL HA\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bk\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backups and PITR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bq\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">BigQuery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sp\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Spanner or Firestore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Cloud SQL HA; 2, Cloud SQL HA to Backups and PITR; 3, Application to Spanner or Firestore; 4, Application to BigQuery.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Connects privately with IAM auth.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Public IPs with passwords invite attacks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sql\">\u003cdt>Cloud SQL HA \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Primary plus standby in another zone.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Failover causes a brief connection drop.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bk\">\u003cdt>Backups and PITR \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Restore to a point in time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An untested restore is only a hope.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bq\">\u003cdt>BigQuery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Analytics on partitioned tables.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A query without a partition filter scans everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sp\">\u003cdt>Spanner or Firestore \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Global scale and special models.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Used where Cloud SQL suffices, they cost more.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-expensive-query\">Incident: The expensive query\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A query with no partition filter scanned a huge table.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bq show -j bqjob_r123\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bq show -j bqjob_r123\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Total bytes processed: 48.2 TB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">table: logs.events (not partitioned)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The table had no partitioning or required filter. The team partitioned by date, set require_partition_filter and added a per-user daily bytes limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read replicas used as high availability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Public database IPs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restores never practised\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select * and no dry run in BigQuery\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-map","text":"The map"},{"id":"cloud-sql-safely","text":"Cloud SQL safely"},{"id":"bigquery","text":"BigQuery"},{"id":"incident-the-expensive-query","text":"Incident: The expensive query"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cloud SQL documentation","u":"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs"},{"t":"BigQuery documentation","u":"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs"}],"text":"Choosing the wrong database is expensive to reverse. A short map of what each Google Cloud database is good at, plus safe defaults for the common one, prevents most regret. What you will learn The database map Cloud SQL high availability and backups Connecting privately BigQuery cost control The map Need Choose Normal relational app Cloud SQL (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server) Heavy PostgreSQL, analytics mix AlloyDB Globally consistent relational, huge scale Spanner Documents, mobile and web Firestore Very high-throughput key-value Bigtable Analytics over large data BigQuery Cloud SQL safely $ gcloud sql instances create orders-db --database-version=POSTGRES_16 --tier=db-custom-4-16384 \\ --region=europe-west1 --availability-type=REGIONAL --network=prod-vpc --no-assign-ip \\ --backup-start-time=02:00 --enable-point-in-time-recovery $ gcloud sql connect orders-db --user=app High availability keeps a standby in another zone with automatic failover. Backups plus point-in-time recovery let you restore to a moment; practise a restore. Use private IP , and the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy or connectors for IAM-based encrypted connections. Add read replicas for read scaling, which are not a substitute for HA. BigQuery BigQuery separates storage from compute and bills by data scanned (on-demand) or by reserved capacity. Partition tables by date, cluster by common filters, select only needed columns and use a dry run. $ bq query --dry_run --use_legacy_sql= false &#x27;select user_id from logs.events where dt = \"2026-11-05\"&#x27; Query successfully validated. This query will process 3.1 GB when run. Picking and protecting a database Flow in order: 1, Application to Cloud SQL HA; 2, Cloud SQL HA to Backups and PITR; 3, Application to Spanner or Firestore; 4, Application to BigQuery. Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Service What it does: Connects privately with IAM auth. If it fails or is weak: Public IPs with passwords invite attacks. Cloud SQL HA Data store What it does: Primary plus standby in another zone. If it fails or is weak: Failover causes a brief connection drop. Backups and PITR Data store What it does: Restore to a point in time. If it fails or is weak: An untested restore is only a hope. BigQuery Data store What it does: Analytics on partitioned tables. If it fails or is weak: A query without a partition filter scans everything. Spanner or Firestore Data store What it does: Global scale and special models. If it fails or is weak: Used where Cloud SQL suffices, they cost more. Incident: The expensive query Incident A query with no partition filter scanned a huge table. $ bq show -j bqjob_r123 Total bytes processed: 48.2 TB table: logs.events (not partitioned) The table had no partitioning or required filter. The team partitioned by date, set require_partition_filter and added a per-user daily bytes limit. Common mistakes Read replicas used as high availability Public database IPs Restores never practised Select * and no dry run in BigQuery"},"gcp/pub-sub-eventarc-and-cloud-tasks":{"html":"\u003cp>Asynchronous messaging links the parts of a Google Cloud system without making them wait for each other. Three services cover the common cases and share the same rules about retries and duplicates.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Topics, subscriptions and acknowledgements\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dead letters and retries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Eventarc and Cloud Scheduler\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud Tasks for targeted work\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pub-sub\">Pub/Sub\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud pubsub topics create orders\ngcloud pubsub subscriptions create billing --topic=orders --ack-deadline=60 \\\n    --dead-letter-topic=orders-dead --max-delivery-attempts=5 \\\n    --message-retention-duration=7d\ngcloud pubsub topics publish orders --message='{&quot;id&quot;:4411}' --attribute=region=eu\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud pubsub topics create orders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud pubsub subscriptions create billing --topic=orders --ack-deadline=60 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --dead-letter-topic=orders-dead --max-delivery-attempts=5 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --message-retention-duration=7d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud pubsub topics publish orders --message=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;id&quot;:4411}&#x27;\u003c/span> --attribute=region=eu\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>topic\u003c/strong> receives messages; each \u003cstrong>subscription\u003c/strong> gets its own copy. \u003cstrong>Pull\u003c/strong> subscribers fetch; \u003cstrong>push\u003c/strong> subscriptions call an HTTPS endpoint such as Cloud Run. Delivery is \u003cstrong>at-least-once\u003c/strong>. If the subscriber does not acknowledge within the \u003cstrong>ack deadline\u003c/strong>, the message is redelivered. After the maximum attempts it goes to the \u003cstrong>dead-letter topic\u003c/strong>. Retained messages and \u003cstrong>seek\u003c/strong> allow replay, and \u003cstrong>ordering keys\u003c/strong> keep messages with one key in order.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"eventarc-and-scheduler\">Eventarc and Scheduler\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Eventarc\u003c/strong> routes events (a file uploaded to Cloud Storage, an audit log entry, a Pub/Sub message) to Cloud Run or functions. \u003cstrong>Cloud Scheduler\u003c/strong> triggers a topic or URL on a cron schedule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cloud-tasks\">Cloud Tasks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Cloud Tasks\u003c/strong> queues individual HTTP tasks for a specific handler, with rate limits (\u003ccode>maxDispatchesPerSecond\u003c/code>), concurrency limits and retry settings. Choose Tasks when you want to control how fast one service is called; choose Pub/Sub when many subscribers need an event.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pub&gt;top;top&gt;sub;sub&gt;dlt;top&gt;evt\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Asynchronous paths on Google Cloud\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pub\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Publisher\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"top\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pub/Sub topic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sub\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Subscription\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dlt\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dead-letter topic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"evt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Eventarc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Publisher to Pub/Sub topic; 2, Pub/Sub topic to Subscription; 3, Subscription to Dead-letter topic; 4, Pub/Sub topic to Eventarc.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pub\">\u003cdt>Publisher \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends events to a topic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A message without an id is hard to trace.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"top\">\u003cdt>Pub/Sub topic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Copies each message to every subscription.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing subscription loses the event for that consumer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sub\">\u003cdt>Subscription \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pull or push delivery with an ack deadline.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Short deadlines cause duplicates.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dlt\">\u003cdt>Dead-letter topic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Collects messages that failed N times.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unwatched, it hides failures.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"evt\">\u003cdt>Eventarc \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Turns Google events into calls to Cloud Run.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong filters send nothing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-redelivered-again-and-again\">Incident: Redelivered again and again\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Messages were delivered repeatedly because handling took longer than the acknowledgement deadline.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud pubsub subscriptions describe billing --format='value(ackDeadlineSeconds)'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud pubsub subscriptions describe billing --format=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;value(ackDeadlineSeconds)&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing log: handler took 45s for message 8812; delivery attempt 4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The handler outlasted the deadline, so Pub/Sub assumed failure. The team raised the deadline, made the handler idempotent and added a dead-letter topic with an alert.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ack deadline shorter than the handler\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No dead-letter topic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handlers that cannot safely run twice\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using Pub/Sub for rate-controlled calls to one service\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"pub-sub","text":"Pub/Sub"},{"id":"eventarc-and-scheduler","text":"Eventarc and Scheduler"},{"id":"cloud-tasks","text":"Cloud Tasks"},{"id":"incident-redelivered-again-and-again","text":"Incident: Redelivered again and again"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pub/Sub overview","u":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview"},{"t":"Eventarc overview","u":"https://cloud.google.com/eventarc/docs/overview"}],"text":"Asynchronous messaging links the parts of a Google Cloud system without making them wait for each other. Three services cover the common cases and share the same rules about retries and duplicates. What you will learn Topics, subscriptions and acknowledgements Dead letters and retries Eventarc and Cloud Scheduler Cloud Tasks for targeted work Pub/Sub $ gcloud pubsub topics create orders $ gcloud pubsub subscriptions create billing --topic=orders --ack-deadline=60 \\ --dead-letter-topic=orders-dead --max-delivery-attempts=5 \\ --message-retention-duration=7d $ gcloud pubsub topics publish orders --message= &#x27;{\"id\":4411}&#x27; --attribute=region=eu A topic receives messages; each subscription gets its own copy. Pull subscribers fetch; push subscriptions call an HTTPS endpoint such as Cloud Run. Delivery is at-least-once . If the subscriber does not acknowledge within the ack deadline , the message is redelivered. After the maximum attempts it goes to the dead-letter topic . Retained messages and seek allow replay, and ordering keys keep messages with one key in order. Eventarc and Scheduler Eventarc routes events (a file uploaded to Cloud Storage, an audit log entry, a Pub/Sub message) to Cloud Run or functions. Cloud Scheduler triggers a topic or URL on a cron schedule. Cloud Tasks Cloud Tasks queues individual HTTP tasks for a specific handler, with rate limits ( maxDispatchesPerSecond ), concurrency limits and retry settings. Choose Tasks when you want to control how fast one service is called; choose Pub/Sub when many subscribers need an event. Asynchronous paths on Google Cloud Flow in order: 1, Publisher to Pub/Sub topic; 2, Pub/Sub topic to Subscription; 3, Subscription to Dead-letter topic; 4, Pub/Sub topic to Eventarc. Step Queue Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Publisher Step What it does: Sends events to a topic. If it fails or is weak: A message without an id is hard to trace. Pub/Sub topic Queue What it does: Copies each message to every subscription. If it fails or is weak: A missing subscription loses the event for that consumer. Subscription Queue What it does: Pull or push delivery with an ack deadline. If it fails or is weak: Short deadlines cause duplicates. Dead-letter topic Observability What it does: Collects messages that failed N times. If it fails or is weak: Unwatched, it hides failures. Eventarc Step What it does: Turns Google events into calls to Cloud Run. If it fails or is weak: Wrong filters send nothing. Incident: Redelivered again and again Incident Messages were delivered repeatedly because handling took longer than the acknowledgement deadline. $ gcloud pubsub subscriptions describe billing --format= &#x27;value(ackDeadlineSeconds)&#x27; 10 billing log: handler took 45s for message 8812; delivery attempt 4 The handler outlasted the deadline, so Pub/Sub assumed failure. The team raised the deadline, made the handler idempotent and added a dead-letter topic with an alert. Common mistakes Ack deadline shorter than the handler No dead-letter topic Handlers that cannot safely run twice Using Pub/Sub for rate-controlled calls to one service"},"gcp/observability-with-cloud-logging-monitoring-and-slos":{"html":"\u003cp>The operations suite is on by default for most services, so the work is to use it well: query logs quickly, make alerts meaningful and measure reliability with an objective.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logs Explorer, sinks and log-based metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Metrics, dashboards and alerting policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Traces, errors and uptime checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SLOs, error budgets and burn-rate alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logs\">Logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud logging read 'resource.type=&quot;cloud_run_revision&quot; AND severity&gt;=ERROR AND timestamp&gt;=&quot;2026-11-05T09:00:00Z&quot;' --limit=20 --format=json\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud logging \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;resource.type=&quot;cloud_run_revision&quot; AND severity&gt;=ERROR AND timestamp&gt;=&quot;2026-11-05T09:00:00Z&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span>=20 --format=json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Query fields, not text, where possible. The \u003cstrong>log router\u003c/strong> sends logs to buckets, \u003cstrong>sinks\u003c/strong> (BigQuery, Cloud Storage, Pub/Sub) and can \u003cstrong>exclude\u003c/strong> noisy entries to reduce cost. \u003cstrong>Log-based metrics\u003c/strong> count matching entries so you can alert on them. Use structured JSON logs with \u003ccode>severity\u003c/code> and a request id.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metrics-and-alerts\">Metrics and alerts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud Monitoring stores metrics for Google services; install the \u003cstrong>Ops Agent\u003c/strong> on VMs, and use \u003cstrong>Managed Service for Prometheus\u003c/strong> for Kubernetes. An \u003cstrong>alerting policy\u003c/strong> has a condition, a duration and \u003cstrong>notification channels\u003c/strong> (email, chat, paging). Add \u003cstrong>uptime checks\u003c/strong> for external probes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traces-and-errors\">Traces and errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Cloud Trace\u003c/strong> shows where a request spent time across services; \u003cstrong>Error Reporting\u003c/strong> groups exceptions; \u003cstrong>Profiler\u003c/strong> finds hot code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"slos\">SLOs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Define a \u003cstrong>service level indicator\u003c/strong> (good requests divided by all requests), an \u003cstrong>objective\u003c/strong> (99.9 percent over 30 days) and the \u003cstrong>error budget\u003c/strong> (0.1 percent). Alert on \u003cstrong>burn rate\u003c/strong>: a fast burn (budget gone in hours) pages someone; a slow burn creates a ticket.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"svc&gt;log;svc&gt;mon;mon&gt;slo;slo&gt;pg\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From signals to action\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Services\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"log\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Monitoring\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"slo\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">SLO and burn rate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">On-call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Services to Logging; 2, Services to Monitoring; 3, Monitoring to SLO and burn rate; 4, SLO and burn rate to On-call.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Services \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Emit logs, metrics and traces.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unstructured logs are hard to query.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"log\">\u003cdt>Logging \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores, routes and filters log entries.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unfiltered debug logs raise cost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Monitoring \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Metrics, dashboards and alerting policies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> CPU-only alerts miss user pain.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"slo\">\u003cdt>SLO and burn rate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compares good requests with the objective.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A target nobody agreed on drives bad decisions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pg\">\u003cdt>On-call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Gets paged for fast burn only.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alert noise causes ignored pages.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-high-cpu-no-pager\">Incident: High CPU, no pager\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A slowdown went unnoticed because the alerts watched CPU while latency tripled.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">monitoring: run.googleapis.com/request_latencies p95 = 2.4s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">monitoring: cpu utilisation = 41%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">alerting policies: cpu &gt; 80% (not firing)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The alert was about a cause, not the user&#39;s experience. A latency SLO with burn-rate alerts replaced the CPU page, and CPU remained on the dashboard for diagnosis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Alerting on causes instead of symptoms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging everything at debug in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner for alert policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SLOs without an agreed error budget policy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"logs","text":"Logs"},{"id":"metrics-and-alerts","text":"Metrics and alerts"},{"id":"traces-and-errors","text":"Traces and errors"},{"id":"slos","text":"SLOs"},{"id":"incident-high-cpu-no-pager","text":"Incident: High CPU, no pager"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cloud Logging","u":"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs"},{"t":"Cloud Monitoring SLOs","u":"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring"}],"text":"The operations suite is on by default for most services, so the work is to use it well: query logs quickly, make alerts meaningful and measure reliability with an objective. What you will learn Logs Explorer, sinks and log-based metrics Metrics, dashboards and alerting policies Traces, errors and uptime checks SLOs, error budgets and burn-rate alerts Logs $ gcloud logging read &#x27;resource.type=\"cloud_run_revision\" AND severity>=ERROR AND timestamp>=\"2026-11-05T09:00:00Z\"&#x27; -- limit =20 --format=json Query fields, not text, where possible. The log router sends logs to buckets, sinks (BigQuery, Cloud Storage, Pub/Sub) and can exclude noisy entries to reduce cost. Log-based metrics count matching entries so you can alert on them. Use structured JSON logs with severity and a request id. Metrics and alerts Cloud Monitoring stores metrics for Google services; install the Ops Agent on VMs, and use Managed Service for Prometheus for Kubernetes. An alerting policy has a condition, a duration and notification channels (email, chat, paging). Add uptime checks for external probes. Traces and errors Cloud Trace shows where a request spent time across services; Error Reporting groups exceptions; Profiler finds hot code. SLOs Define a service level indicator (good requests divided by all requests), an objective (99.9 percent over 30 days) and the error budget (0.1 percent). Alert on burn rate : a fast burn (budget gone in hours) pages someone; a slow burn creates a ticket. From signals to action Flow in order: 1, Services to Logging; 2, Services to Monitoring; 3, Monitoring to SLO and burn rate; 4, SLO and burn rate to On-call. Service Observability Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Services Service What it does: Emit logs, metrics and traces. If it fails or is weak: Unstructured logs are hard to query. Logging Observability What it does: Stores, routes and filters log entries. If it fails or is weak: Unfiltered debug logs raise cost. Monitoring Observability What it does: Metrics, dashboards and alerting policies. If it fails or is weak: CPU-only alerts miss user pain. SLO and burn rate Security What it does: Compares good requests with the objective. If it fails or is weak: A target nobody agreed on drives bad decisions. On-call Step What it does: Gets paged for fast burn only. If it fails or is weak: Alert noise causes ignored pages. Incident: High CPU, no pager Incident A slowdown went unnoticed because the alerts watched CPU while latency tripled. monitoring: run.googleapis.com/request_latencies p95 = 2.4s monitoring: cpu utilisation = 41% alerting policies: cpu > 80% (not firing) The alert was about a cause, not the user&#39;s experience. A latency SLO with burn-rate alerts replaced the CPU page, and CPU remained on the dashboard for diagnosis. Common mistakes Alerting on causes instead of symptoms Logging everything at debug in production No owner for alert policies SLOs without an agreed error budget policy"},"gcp/terraform-on-google-cloud":{"html":"\u003cp>Terraform is the most common way to manage Google Cloud as code. The provider is simple; the work is in state, identity and structure, which decide whether it stays safe as the team grows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Provider, backend and authentication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remote state in Cloud Storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pipelines with federated identity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Structure: environments, modules and project factories\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"provider-and-state\">Provider and state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform {\n  required_providers {\n    google = { source = &quot;hashicorp/google&quot;, version = &quot;~&gt; 6.0&quot; }\n  }\n  backend &quot;gcs&quot; {\n    bucket = &quot;acme-tfstate-prod&quot;\n    prefix = &quot;network&quot;\n  }\n}\n\nprovider &quot;google&quot; {\n  project = var.project_id\n  region  = &quot;europe-west1&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;google_compute_network&quot; &quot;vpc&quot; {\n  name                    = &quot;prod-vpc&quot;\n  auto_create_subnetworks = false\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">terraform\u003c/span> {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">required_providers\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    google\u003c/span> = { source = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hashicorp/google&quot;\u003c/span>, version = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;~&gt; 6.0&quot;\u003c/span> }\n  }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">backend\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;gcs&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    bucket\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-tfstate-prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    prefix\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;network&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">provider\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;google&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  project\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.project_id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  region\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;europe-west1&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;google_compute_network&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vpc&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>                    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod-vpc&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  auto_create_subnetworks\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>gcs\u003c/code> backend stores state in a bucket with locking. Turn on \u003cstrong>object versioning\u003c/strong> so you can recover a bad state, restrict access to the bucket, and use \u003cstrong>one state per layer\u003c/strong> (network, data, apps) to limit blast radius.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authentication\">Authentication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Locally use \u003ccode>gcloud auth application-default login\u003c/code>, or better \u003ccode>GOOGLE_IMPERSONATE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=tf-deployer@...\u003c/code>. In CI use \u003cstrong>Workload Identity Federation\u003c/strong> so GitHub or GitLab exchanges its token for the service account with no key stored.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"structure\">Structure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use modules for patterns (a standard project, a VPC), a \u003cstrong>project factory\u003c/strong> module to create projects with budgets, APIs and service accounts, and review \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> output on pull requests. \u003cstrong>Config Connector\u003c/strong> manages Google resources from Kubernetes objects, and \u003cstrong>Infrastructure Manager\u003c/strong> runs Terraform as a Google-managed service.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pr&gt;ci;ci&gt;wif;wif&gt;state;wif&gt;gcp\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Terraform pipeline on Google Cloud\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pull request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CI job\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wif\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workload Identity Federation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"state\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">State bucket\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gcp\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Google Cloud resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pull request to CI job; 2, CI job to Workload Identity Federation; 3, Workload Identity Federation to State bucket; 4, Workload Identity Federation to Google Cloud resources.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pr\">\u003cdt>Pull request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Plan output reviewed by people.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unreviewed plan can destroy resources.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>CI job \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs fmt, validate, plan and apply.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long-lived keys in settings can leak.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wif\">\u003cdt>Workload Identity Federation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Token exchange for a narrow service account.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A broad role defeats the benefit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"state\">\u003cdt>State bucket \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Versioned and locked remote state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Public or shared access exposes secrets in state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gcp\">\u003cdt>Google Cloud resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The projects, networks and services managed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Console edits cause drift.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-leaked-key\">Incident: The leaked key\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service account key in pipeline settings was exposed with the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud iam service-accounts keys list --iam-account=tf-deployer@acme.iam.gserviceaccount.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud iam service-accounts keys list --iam-account=tf-deployer@acme.iam.gserviceaccount.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">KEY_ID  CREATED_AT  2024-06-12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">audit log: tf-deployer from 203.0.113.9 (unknown)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key had wide rights and no expiry. The team deleted it, moved the pipeline to federation, narrowed the role per layer and enabled an organization policy blocking new key creation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Committing keys or state to Git\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One giant state for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A single owner-level deployer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A state bucket without versioning or access limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"provider-and-state","text":"Provider and state"},{"id":"authentication","text":"Authentication"},{"id":"structure","text":"Structure"},{"id":"incident-the-leaked-key","text":"Incident: The leaked key"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform on Google Cloud","u":"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform"},{"t":"Google provider documentation","u":"https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/google/latest/docs"}],"text":"Terraform is the most common way to manage Google Cloud as code. The provider is simple; the work is in state, identity and structure, which decide whether it stays safe as the team grows. What you will learn Provider, backend and authentication Remote state in Cloud Storage Pipelines with federated identity Structure: environments, modules and project factories Provider and state terraform { required_providers { google = { source = \"hashicorp/google\" , version = \"~> 6.0\" } } backend \"gcs\" { bucket = \"acme-tfstate-prod\" prefix = \"network\" } } provider \"google\" { project = var .project_id region = \"europe-west1\" } resource \"google_compute_network\" \"vpc\" { name = \"prod-vpc\" auto_create_subnetworks = false } The gcs backend stores state in a bucket with locking. Turn on object versioning so you can recover a bad state, restrict access to the bucket, and use one state per layer (network, data, apps) to limit blast radius. Authentication Locally use gcloud auth application-default login , or better GOOGLE_IMPERSONATE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=tf-deployer@... . In CI use Workload Identity Federation so GitHub or GitLab exchanges its token for the service account with no key stored. Structure Use modules for patterns (a standard project, a VPC), a project factory module to create projects with budgets, APIs and service accounts, and review terraform plan output on pull requests. Config Connector manages Google resources from Kubernetes objects, and Infrastructure Manager runs Terraform as a Google-managed service. Terraform pipeline on Google Cloud Flow in order: 1, Pull request to CI job; 2, CI job to Workload Identity Federation; 3, Workload Identity Federation to State bucket; 4, Workload Identity Federation to Google Cloud resources. Step Service Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pull request Step What it does: Plan output reviewed by people. If it fails or is weak: An unreviewed plan can destroy resources. CI job Service What it does: Runs fmt, validate, plan and apply. If it fails or is weak: Long-lived keys in settings can leak. Workload Identity Federation Security What it does: Token exchange for a narrow service account. If it fails or is weak: A broad role defeats the benefit. State bucket Data store What it does: Versioned and locked remote state. If it fails or is weak: Public or shared access exposes secrets in state. Google Cloud resources Data store What it does: The projects, networks and services managed. If it fails or is weak: Console edits cause drift. Incident: The leaked key Incident A service account key in pipeline settings was exposed with the repository. $ gcloud iam service-accounts keys list --iam-account=tf-deployer@acme.iam.gserviceaccount.com KEY_ID CREATED_AT 2024-06-12 audit log: tf-deployer from 203.0.113.9 (unknown) The key had wide rights and no expiry. The team deleted it, moved the pipeline to federation, narrowed the role per layer and enabled an organization policy blocking new key creation. Common mistakes Committing keys or state to Git One giant state for everything A single owner-level deployer A state bucket without versioning or access limits"},"gcp/security-cost-and-debugging-on-google-cloud":{"html":"\u003cp>Google Cloud is secure by design but easy to misconfigure. The same short list of controls shows up in nearly every well-run organisation, and a repeatable debugging routine saves hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>IAM least privilege and recommender\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Organization policies and VPC Service Controls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audit logs and cost control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A routine for permission, quota and API errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"iam\">IAM\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Grant \u003cstrong>predefined roles\u003c/strong> to groups at the lowest level (bucket or project, not organization). Avoid basic roles Owner, Editor and Viewer in production. Use \u003cstrong>IAM Recommender\u003c/strong> to remove unused permissions and \u003cstrong>conditions\u003c/strong> for time-limited access. Service accounts get one job each.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"organization-policies-and-perimeters\">Organization policies and perimeters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Constraints enforced across the tree: disable service account key creation, require OS Login, restrict public IPs, restrict resource locations, enforce public access prevention. \u003cstrong>VPC Service Controls\u003c/strong> draw a perimeter around sensitive services (BigQuery, Cloud Storage) so data cannot be copied out even with valid credentials from outside the perimeter. \u003cstrong>Cloud Audit Logs\u003c/strong> record admin activity (always on) and data access (enable for sensitive data). \u003cstrong>Security Command Center\u003c/strong> surfaces misconfigurations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost\">Cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set budgets and alerts, export billing to BigQuery, label resources, rightsize with recommendations, delete idle disks and IPs, use committed use discounts for steady load and Spot for tolerant jobs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debugging-errors\">Debugging errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Error\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>First step\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PERMISSION_DENIED\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Identity lacks a permission\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Policy Troubleshooter for the exact permission\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>API has not been used\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Service not enabled in the project\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>gcloud services enable\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED\u003c/code> / quota\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Limit reached\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Check quotas page; request an increase or change region\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Request is prohibited by organization&#39;s policy\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Org policy denies\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>View the constraint and use the approved path\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>VPC-SC violation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Perimeter blocked the call\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Read the audit log violation entry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"err&gt;kind;kind&gt;pt;kind&gt;log;pt&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Debugging a denied request\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"err\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Error message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kind\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Which kind\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pt\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy Troubleshooter\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"log\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Smallest fix\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Error message to Which kind; 2, Which kind to Policy Troubleshooter; 3, Which kind to Audit logs; 4, Policy Troubleshooter to Smallest fix.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"err\">\u003cdt>Error message \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Names the code and often the permission.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skimming the message hides the real cause.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kind\">\u003cdt>Which kind \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Permission, API, quota, policy or perimeter.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong guess leads to a wrong fix.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pt\">\u003cdt>Policy Troubleshooter \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows which role would allow the call.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Granting Owner hides the real gap.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"log\">\u003cdt>Audit logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Show the caller, method and denial reason.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Disabled data-access logs hide reads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Smallest fix \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Grant a narrow role or enable the API.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Broad grants widen the blast radius.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-owner-to-make-it-work\">Incident: Owner to make it work\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A permission error was silenced by granting Owner to a developer.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud projects get-iam-policy acme-pay-prod --flatten=bindings --filter=bindings.role:roles/owner\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud projects get-iam-policy acme-pay-prod --flatten=bindings --filter=bindings.role:roles/owner\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">user:dev@acme.example  roles/owner\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">troubleshooter: missing storage.objects.get on bucket acme-exports\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The developer needed one read permission on one bucket. The team removed Owner, granted the narrow bucket role and enabled IAM Recommender reviews every quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting Owner or Editor to fix errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Service account keys left in the wild\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No budgets or labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No data-access audit logging for sensitive data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"iam","text":"IAM"},{"id":"organization-policies-and-perimeters","text":"Organization policies and perimeters"},{"id":"cost","text":"Cost"},{"id":"debugging-errors","text":"Debugging errors"},{"id":"incident-owner-to-make-it-work","text":"Incident: Owner to make it work"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"IAM best practices","u":"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely"},{"t":"VPC Service Controls","u":"https://cloud.google.com/vpc-service-controls/docs/overview"}],"text":"Google Cloud is secure by design but easy to misconfigure. The same short list of controls shows up in nearly every well-run organisation, and a repeatable debugging routine saves hours. What you will learn IAM least privilege and recommender Organization policies and VPC Service Controls Audit logs and cost control A routine for permission, quota and API errors IAM Grant predefined roles to groups at the lowest level (bucket or project, not organization). Avoid basic roles Owner, Editor and Viewer in production. Use IAM Recommender to remove unused permissions and conditions for time-limited access. Service accounts get one job each. Organization policies and perimeters Constraints enforced across the tree: disable service account key creation, require OS Login, restrict public IPs, restrict resource locations, enforce public access prevention. VPC Service Controls draw a perimeter around sensitive services (BigQuery, Cloud Storage) so data cannot be copied out even with valid credentials from outside the perimeter. Cloud Audit Logs record admin activity (always on) and data access (enable for sensitive data). Security Command Center surfaces misconfigurations. Cost Set budgets and alerts, export billing to BigQuery, label resources, rightsize with recommendations, delete idle disks and IPs, use committed use discounts for steady load and Spot for tolerant jobs. Debugging errors Error Meaning First step PERMISSION_DENIED Identity lacks a permission Policy Troubleshooter for the exact permission API has not been used Service not enabled in the project gcloud services enable RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED / quota Limit reached Check quotas page; request an increase or change region Request is prohibited by organization&#39;s policy Org policy denies View the constraint and use the approved path VPC-SC violation Perimeter blocked the call Read the audit log violation entry Debugging a denied request Flow in order: 1, Error message to Which kind; 2, Which kind to Policy Troubleshooter; 3, Which kind to Audit logs; 4, Policy Troubleshooter to Smallest fix. Queue Step Observability Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Error message Queue What it does: Names the code and often the permission. If it fails or is weak: Skimming the message hides the real cause. Which kind Step What it does: Permission, API, quota, policy or perimeter. If it fails or is weak: A wrong guess leads to a wrong fix. Policy Troubleshooter Observability What it does: Shows which role would allow the call. If it fails or is weak: Granting Owner hides the real gap. Audit logs Observability What it does: Show the caller, method and denial reason. If it fails or is weak: Disabled data-access logs hide reads. Smallest fix Security What it does: Grant a narrow role or enable the API. If it fails or is weak: Broad grants widen the blast radius. Incident: Owner to make it work Incident A permission error was silenced by granting Owner to a developer. $ gcloud projects get-iam-policy acme-pay-prod --flatten=bindings --filter=bindings.role:roles/owner user:dev@acme.example roles/owner troubleshooter: missing storage.objects.get on bucket acme-exports The developer needed one read permission on one bucket. The team removed Owner, granted the narrow bucket role and enabled IAM Recommender reviews every quarter. Common mistakes Granting Owner or Editor to fix errors Service account keys left in the wild No budgets or labels No data-access audit logging for sensitive data"}});